00:14:26,560 --> 00:14:28,960
and we're still talking about a lot of things.

228
00:14:28,960 --> 00:14:33,120
However, I do feel like there's a lot more clarity in how stablecoins are going to be

229
00:14:33,120 --> 00:14:34,120
viewed.

230
00:14:34,120 --> 00:14:36,760
You know, the FCA, for example, we're going to look at them as a crypto asset and not

231
00:14:36,760 --> 00:14:41,920
as e-money and making those differentiations has been really important for not only us

232
00:14:41,920 --> 00:14:46,280
as an organisation, but also a lot of the businesses that we want to face off because

233
00:14:46,280 --> 00:14:51,400
really at the end of the day, they can have as much innovation and be as excited about

234
00:14:51,440 --> 00:14:55,400
stablecoins as they like, but if their compliance and legal departments aren't willing to sign

235
00:14:55,400 --> 00:14:59,000
off, then it's not going to go very far.

236
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,880
I guess to sum it up, we don't have any regulation at the moment.

237
00:15:02,880 --> 00:15:09,920
We're trying to fit stablecoins into existing registrations that currently exist and I think

238
00:15:09,920 --> 00:15:15,360
that'll be about a year or two away before we get any dedicated UK stablecoin regulations.

239
00:15:15,360 --> 00:15:21,080
So I guess at the moment we can backdoor through anti-money laundering registrations.

240
00:15:21,080 --> 00:15:25,600
So to speak, but I would say there is a little bit of hostility still there, but there's

241
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:28,360
been a huge shift in the tone, which is what is really positive.

242
00:15:28,360 --> 00:15:33,840
A couple of our advisors were at some roundtables at Point Zero last week and there was a lot

243
00:15:33,840 --> 00:15:38,240
of central bankers there and again, they said the tone of the conversation and the level

244
00:15:38,240 --> 00:15:43,160
of understanding is quite exciting and making them feel quite optimistic.

245
00:15:43,160 --> 00:15:49,160
There's definitely a shift to being pro-innovation, crypto-friendly and trying to be one of the

246
00:15:49,160 --> 00:15:53,560
first movers within this as we go forward.

247
00:15:53,560 --> 00:15:57,880
You must have some interesting conversations with powerful institutions because I imagine

248
00:15:57,880 --> 00:16:02,240
you speak to banks about all the things you guys want to do and are excited to do and

249
00:16:02,240 --> 00:16:04,760
you're probably in some ways worried.

250
00:16:04,760 --> 00:16:07,840
What if they decide they want to issue a GBP stablecoin?

251
00:16:07,840 --> 00:16:09,960
Does that cross your mind?

252
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:11,960
It's a question that comes up almost every day.

253
00:16:11,960 --> 00:16:14,560
Someone says, what if the large incumbents come in?

254
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:18,800
You can almost split that conversation up into a number of different buckets, so to

255
00:16:18,800 --> 00:16:19,800
speak.

256
00:16:19,800 --> 00:16:25,240
If you look at banks at the moment, there's a real worry from the PRA, which is under

257
00:16:25,240 --> 00:16:30,600
the Bank of England, the Financial Regulatory Authority, that regulated businesses could

258
00:16:30,600 --> 00:16:35,480
start offering unregulated financial products.

259
00:16:35,480 --> 00:16:39,480
They're really saying to banks, we need you to wait until we have the clarity that we

260
00:16:39,480 --> 00:16:43,200
need until we have the regulation before we can have you start doing this.

261
00:16:43,520 --> 00:16:46,880
It's not as though that the banks don't want to, it's that they can.

262
00:16:46,880 --> 00:16:52,520
We've been making the most of that, but then if you look at existing stablecoin businesses

263
00:16:52,520 --> 00:16:57,400
and organizations, I think a lot of them, with the conversations that we've had, they

264
00:16:57,400 --> 00:17:02,160
feel like there's still so much for them to go after doing what they're doing at the moment

265
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:04,080
within the dollar economy itself.

266
00:17:04,080 --> 00:17:08,960
There's still so much growth, so we're operating under the radar.

267
00:17:08,960 --> 00:17:14,480
I think there's a lot more traction within the UK and within GBP within the last three,

268
00:17:14,480 --> 00:17:16,960
four months than there was even last year.

269
00:17:16,960 --> 00:17:19,520
The tide is really starting to turn.

270
00:17:19,520 --> 00:17:24,360
Will they allow this innovation to exist, the British regulatory bodies?

271
00:17:24,360 --> 00:17:29,520
I would say the folks are probably lobbying there, or do you think there will be pushback?

272
00:17:29,520 --> 00:17:31,560
I don't think there'll be pushback from the banks.

273
00:17:31,560 --> 00:17:34,160
I don't think there'll be pushback from the industry itself.

274
00:17:34,160 --> 00:17:38,880
I mean, UK financial institutions have always been quite open to innovation.

275
00:17:38,880 --> 00:17:40,280
That's refreshing.

276
00:17:40,280 --> 00:17:41,280
I wish we had that.

277
00:17:41,280 --> 00:17:42,280
Yeah.

278
00:17:42,280 --> 00:17:43,280
Maybe that's a slightly controversial view.

279
00:17:43,280 --> 00:17:44,280
I'm not sure.

280
00:17:44,280 --> 00:17:48,680
But if you look at, for example, payments innovation in the UK, we were very quick to

281
00:17:48,680 --> 00:17:50,680
move to mobile banking, for example.

282
00:17:50,680 --> 00:17:54,200
We were one of the first countries to start using contactless with the widespread adoption

283
00:17:54,200 --> 00:17:55,200
that we had.

284
00:17:55,200 --> 00:17:59,860
So people are quite open to using new forms of money and new forms of payment systems.

285
00:17:59,860 --> 00:18:03,880
So I think financial institutions especially can see the advantages.

286
00:18:03,880 --> 00:18:07,280
I think a lot of them were going to do it earlier in 2022.

287
00:18:07,280 --> 00:18:10,280
I know there was a large UK bank that was looking at doing their own stablecoin and

288
00:18:10,280 --> 00:18:17,120
actually offering cryptocurrency purchases within their own online banking app.

289
00:18:17,120 --> 00:18:21,920
But then FTX happened and the compliance department just said, hell no, we can't do any of this

290
00:18:21,920 --> 00:18:22,920
ourselves.

291
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:26,400
So it's not that they don't want to do it, it's that they can't at the moment.

292
00:18:26,400 --> 00:18:30,080
How important is it that you guys are a UK based company?

293
00:18:30,080 --> 00:18:35,960
I think it's one of the most important parts for us at the moment, getting a local regulator

294
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:41,640
on site, you need to have a local presence, as particularly with the FCA authorizations

295
00:18:41,640 --> 00:18:46,920
are globally recognized as one of the most trusted and respected regulators globally.

296
00:18:46,920 --> 00:18:51,360
It gives us a few benefits, it enables us to conduct a few more activities, some of

297
00:18:51,360 --> 00:18:55,000
which require local authorizations to do those activities.

298
00:18:55,000 --> 00:19:00,480
But typically the regulator likes to use local authorization as a condition for being able

299
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:05,920
to undertake certain types of business, particularly when it comes to facing off retail customers.

300
00:19:05,960 --> 00:19:10,000
And not only that, it gives UK institutions a lot of confidence.

301
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:14,360
The city is one of these strange places where if you come knocking on their door and you're

302
00:19:14,360 --> 00:19:19,840
regulated through one of the slightly more grayer jurisdictions, their tails are automatically

303
00:19:19,840 --> 00:19:24,000
going to be up and they're going to be saying, hang on, why we tried to circumvent what's

304
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:25,000
already there.

305
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:28,400
It gives us the ability to get partnerships, particularly with compliance teams.

306
00:19:28,400 --> 00:19:31,160
Yeah, I imagine there's a comfort level that comes with it.

307
00:19:31,160 --> 00:19:34,840
I'm curious for your perspective, being a stablecoin issuer that is the traditional

308
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:37,000
business of net interest margin.

309
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:40,680
You make money on deposits, especially in the context in the UK.

310
00:19:40,680 --> 00:19:42,920
You've had innovation in the fintech space.

311
00:19:42,920 --> 00:19:44,600
You've seen new players in mobile banking.

312
00:19:44,600 --> 00:19:46,160
You've seen new business models.

313
00:19:46,160 --> 00:19:52,760
How do you look at your best viable business model long term or even more widely?

314
00:19:52,760 --> 00:19:58,000
What the best business model for someone taking on this opportunity of new currency stablecoin?

315
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,400
The net interest margin is always going to be a factor.

316
00:20:00,400 --> 00:20:04,880
I think what's lucky for us at the moment is that the FCA has said that no interest

317
00:20:04,880 --> 00:20:07,240
shall be given to the token holder.

318
00:20:07,240 --> 00:20:11,320
They don't want yield bearing stablecoins to be a thing.

319
00:20:11,320 --> 00:20:12,320
Interesting.

320
00:20:12,320 --> 00:20:13,320
They proactively said that.

321
00:20:13,320 --> 00:20:14,320
Yeah.

322
00:20:14,320 --> 00:20:17,240
They all kind of at the moment want one to always be one.

323
00:20:17,240 --> 00:20:22,400
However, I think as our business develops and changes and grows, we don't want to necessarily

324
00:20:22,400 --> 00:20:26,960
just be relying on that because you're basically one line within a piece of regulation away

325
00:20:26,960 --> 00:20:30,080
from your entire business model no longer existing.

326
00:20:30,080 --> 00:20:32,280
We see a lot on the transaction fee side.

327
00:20:32,280 --> 00:20:35,960
We actually see a lot on the banking element as well.

328
00:20:35,960 --> 00:20:42,040
If you're able to access deposited funds, you can potentially offer banking services

329
00:20:42,040 --> 00:20:46,820
to corporates like lending payments, but then also on the yield bearing side as well.

330
00:20:46,820 --> 00:20:48,920
We see a lot of opportunity there.

331
00:20:48,920 --> 00:20:56,120
I think regulators are more open to a new bank than an organization like us progressing

332
00:20:57,120 --> 00:21:03,300
that looks very like a bank as opposed to an existing bank starting to offer new forms

333
00:21:03,300 --> 00:21:04,300
of business.

334
00:21:04,300 --> 00:21:08,140
You know, they're a little bit cagey about the contagion risk that sits there.

335
00:21:08,140 --> 00:21:12,120
So UK banks have real uphill battle with regulators and that's something that I think we as an

336
00:21:12,120 --> 00:21:15,040
organization can capture within our own business model.

337
00:21:15,040 --> 00:21:19,760
So I think over time, we would quite like a Gantt to start looking more like a bank

338
00:21:19,760 --> 00:21:23,800
and the types of banking services that we'd be able to offer, we can generate revenue

339
00:21:23,800 --> 00:21:25,000
from that.

340
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:30,600
With regulatory considerations in mind, how do you look at launching on private blockchains

341
00:21:30,600 --> 00:21:33,120
versus permissionless blockchains?

342
00:21:33,120 --> 00:21:39,080
I think they all have their place and we've talked to some asset managers that want to

343
00:21:39,080 --> 00:21:42,920
solve, you know, use stablecoins to solve an internal use case.

344
00:21:42,920 --> 00:21:47,080
And so whether that's on a public ledger or private ledger is kind of neither here nor

345
00:21:47,080 --> 00:21:48,080
there.

346
00:21:48,080 --> 00:21:53,760
However, I am a strong believer that we should be on public blockchains as much as we can.

347
00:21:53,760 --> 00:21:58,480
And I think when you see a lot of the innovations in being able to, you know, hide transactions

348
00:21:58,480 --> 00:22:04,560
and shield vulnerabilities away, we want a degree of transparency, but there are times

349
00:22:04,560 --> 00:22:07,920
and places where that transparency isn't good.

350
00:22:07,920 --> 00:22:11,080
There's certain networks out there that are really innovating in that space and making

351
00:22:11,080 --> 00:22:14,440
it quite exciting for us to be a part of that.

352
00:22:14,440 --> 00:22:19,340
And going to institutions, we can see the value proposition of both.

353
00:22:19,340 --> 00:22:23,160
That's a personal viewpoint, to be clear, the preference for permissionless blockchains.

354
00:22:23,560 --> 00:22:24,920
No, that is a personal preference.

355
00:22:24,920 --> 00:22:30,720
I mean, I think there's a lot of advantages to public permissionless technology.

356
00:22:30,720 --> 00:22:34,800
What other product directions are you excited about, potential adjacencies?

357
00:22:34,800 --> 00:22:42,040
We get really excited about being able to generate sustainable risk-adjusted returns.

358
00:22:42,040 --> 00:22:47,520
And I know that sounds quite a mouthful, but I feel like a lot of the returns in DeFi aren't

359
00:22:47,520 --> 00:22:49,760
particularly sustainable long term.

360
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:55,840
I think being able to bring real world returns and distribute them on chain, you know, things

361
00:22:55,840 --> 00:22:57,800
get quite exciting.

362
00:22:57,800 --> 00:23:01,960
It's quite apparent that if you're going to take financial services on chain, the risk

363
00:23:01,960 --> 00:23:04,640
free rate isn't necessarily attractive enough.

364
00:23:04,640 --> 00:23:09,800
And so you need to be offering something that is slightly more enhanced, a few percent more.

365
00:23:09,800 --> 00:23:13,040
So being able to do that is something that we get quite excited about.

366
00:23:13,040 --> 00:23:16,200
But then there's different aspects, you know, I was having a conversation earlier today

367
00:23:16,400 --> 00:23:19,840
about, you know, effectively creating, you know, funds of funds where, you know, you

368
00:23:19,840 --> 00:23:21,440
can diversify that risk.

369
00:23:21,440 --> 00:23:26,600
So, yeah, risk weighted returns on chain where people can see, you know, enhanced returns

370
00:23:26,600 --> 00:23:29,320
without necessarily having to take on incredible amounts of risk.

371
00:23:29,320 --> 00:23:32,360
I personally get quite excited about that side of things.

372
00:23:32,360 --> 00:23:37,080
Yeah, it's interesting because I feel like we've built two opposite sides of the coin

373
00:23:37,080 --> 00:23:41,480
in that you have savings accounts, almost traditional bank accounts, which allow you

374
00:23:41,480 --> 00:23:42,720
to hold money very well.

375
00:23:42,720 --> 00:23:45,880
And then you have the highly speculative DeFi yield side.

376
00:23:46,040 --> 00:23:48,840
But the middle ground is not that well served.

377
00:23:48,840 --> 00:23:53,640
You've Goldman Sachs high yield savings accounts and some equivalents, but it still feels like,

378
00:23:53,640 --> 00:23:58,640
to your point, attractive, steady rate investment products are somewhat gate kept.

379
00:23:58,640 --> 00:24:04,560
100% and a lot of these organizations and funds that maybe offer these financial products,

380
00:24:04,560 --> 00:24:08,040
like you say, you know, the minimum ticket might be 10 million pounds.

381
00:24:08,040 --> 00:24:12,600
We were able to come in and we were able to act as the LP for those organizations and

382
00:24:12,600 --> 00:24:16,880
then we can effectively fractionalize our stake within them.

383
00:24:16,880 --> 00:24:22,000
And we're effectively democratizing those returns because in reality, if you're able

384
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:27,320
to take, let's say, you know, somewhere between 8 and 12% annually, the returns are completely

385
00:24:27,320 --> 00:24:31,240
uncorrelated to financial markets and the level of risk that you're taking is significantly

386
00:24:31,240 --> 00:24:34,280
lower than that within, you know, traditional financial markets.

387
00:24:34,280 --> 00:24:37,800
And that's quite an attractive proposition for a lot of people in a lot of organizations.

388
00:24:38,520 --> 00:24:43,720
If you look at USD stablecoins and how they eventually came to what I'd consider now

389
00:24:43,720 --> 00:24:47,720
rapidly growing adoption, there was a pretty slow bleed for a while.

390
00:24:47,720 --> 00:24:54,040
You had them come into existence and it took a while to get to 10 million, 20 million in total supply.

391
00:24:54,920 --> 00:25:00,520
Do you think that will be the same process we see with a GBP stablecoin and other alternatives?

392
00:25:00,520 --> 00:25:04,120
And then we'll have an inflection and that's for rapid growth we see with USD today?

393
00:25:04,760 --> 00:25:05,720
Not really.

394
00:25:05,720 --> 00:25:10,440
When we launch hopefully in the next few months, I don't think our growth will be by any means linear.

395
00:25:11,080 --> 00:25:15,800
But I don't think it will be slow initially because I do think there is a base level demand

396
00:25:15,800 --> 00:25:19,880
that really wants a financial product on chain that isn't necessarily dollars.

397
00:25:19,880 --> 00:25:25,160
I think initially for us to get to, you know, a circle or a teller kind of size, then yeah,

398
00:25:25,160 --> 00:25:29,400
you know, that's going to take a while and there probably will need to be an inflection point that

399
00:25:29,400 --> 00:25:31,720
we would consider as reaching mass adoption.

400
00:25:32,440 --> 00:25:36,280
However, for us to get to a modest size with a few commas and zeros,

401
00:25:36,840 --> 00:25:39,880
I honestly don't believe that that's going to be particularly long for us.

402
00:25:39,880 --> 00:25:43,400
You know, the types of conversations that we're having and the types of organizations

403
00:25:43,400 --> 00:25:47,960
that are reaching out to us demonstrates that there is this demand.

404
00:25:47,960 --> 00:25:50,920
You know, I think that's one thing that has been quite surprising

405
00:25:51,640 --> 00:25:57,000
is how easily we've been able to have a lot of the conversations that we're currently having.

406
00:25:57,640 --> 00:26:01,480
What Dollar Stablecoins has done for us is they demonstrate the proof isn't pudding.

407
00:26:02,040 --> 00:26:05,640
What's also quite helpful for us is that global financial markets,

408
00:26:05,640 --> 00:26:09,640
while the majority is dollars, there's a lot of value that's also not.

409
00:26:10,200 --> 00:26:15,480
The UK financial ecosystem, you know, the whole industry, a lot of that's not dollars,

410
00:26:15,480 --> 00:26:19,000
but a lot of the use cases can be applied directly to those organizations.

411
00:26:19,640 --> 00:26:25,320
We're feeling pretty optimistic about our trajectory and also that for the industry as a whole.

412
00:26:25,960 --> 00:26:29,480
To your point, I think there is and will continue to be demand.

413
00:26:30,200 --> 00:26:32,120
Who do you think are the winners and losers

414
00:26:32,120 --> 00:26:35,480
once you guys are at scale and other comparable players are at scale?

415
00:26:36,280 --> 00:26:41,640
I sat on a panel a few weeks ago in front of a bunch of bankers and I basically said, you know,

416
00:26:41,640 --> 00:26:44,200
you need to start embracing this or you're going to become a victim of it.

417
00:26:44,760 --> 00:26:49,400
I would say if I was any financial institution, you know, the investment banks are the investment

418
00:26:49,400 --> 00:26:54,200
banks. On the whole, if they manage their risk, they're going to survive in one firm or another.

419
00:26:54,280 --> 00:26:58,920
Where I would be nervous is if I was within the retail banking system.

420
00:26:59,480 --> 00:27:05,640
If I'm an individual within the UK, for example, and I have my savings with a standard high street

421
00:27:05,640 --> 00:27:11,240
retail bank, I'm only covered up to £85,000. Anything more than that, I'm subject to the

422
00:27:11,240 --> 00:27:16,200
bank's own risks and its own reserves. They're fractionally backed. And if I could be fully

423
00:27:16,200 --> 00:27:20,120
backed, I would choose to be fully backed, not fractionally backed. So yeah, I think the UK

424
00:27:20,120 --> 00:27:25,560
retail banking system is probably the most at risk, although I'm sure the regulator will find

425
00:27:25,560 --> 00:27:29,400
ways to protect them if they need to. Presumably these yield-bearing,

426
00:27:29,400 --> 00:27:33,880
inter-spring products will be interesting for them though, at least as business models that they can

427
00:27:33,880 --> 00:27:39,400
almost leach onto. I think so. And I think maybe that's what we start to do is view them as a

428
00:27:39,400 --> 00:27:45,000
distribution channel, look at how we can integrate. I do think that there'll come a point where a lot

429
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:48,360
of these financial institutions will turn around and say, well, if we can't beat them, we'll join

430
00:27:48,360 --> 00:27:54,760
them. Otherwise they will be put under significant pressure. Longer term, do you guys want to be one

431
00:27:54,760 --> 00:28:00,440
flagship product, more akin to a circle, or do you want to be a suite of products or somewhere in the

432
00:28:00,440 --> 00:28:05,000
middle when it comes to how many tokens and on-chain offerings you have? I think we're

433
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:10,280
organically finding that out ourselves. I mean, we initially wanted to start as this retail on

434
00:28:10,280 --> 00:28:16,520
and off ramp that was integrated with open banking and automated KYC and AML to make this seamless

435
00:28:16,520 --> 00:28:23,080
interoperability between Be It and Pounds on Chain. And I guess as we've sort of developed

436
00:28:23,080 --> 00:28:28,920
as individuals and as an organization, we've seen that change between client demand and also

437
00:28:28,920 --> 00:28:35,160
our own ideas. I think yield products on chain make a lot of sense. And I guess we've kind of

438
00:28:35,160 --> 00:28:40,200
seen that with what Circle's doing with their acquisition of Passional. I think for us, we will

439
00:28:40,200 --> 00:28:46,200
have quite a narrow suite of products. We would rather have quality over quantity. I think there's

440
00:28:46,200 --> 00:28:53,400
a requirement for stablecoin issuers to not just be an issuer and to be a really good quality

441
00:28:53,400 --> 00:28:58,360
infrastructure provider as well. Because at the end of the day, if you want organizations to

442
00:28:58,360 --> 00:29:02,760
integrate and build on top of your product, you need to make it particularly easy and attractive

443
00:29:02,760 --> 00:29:08,760
for them to do that work. I wanted to ask you one final question, which is what is the user

444
00:29:08,760 --> 00:29:13,320
behavior change you're seeing at the moment that makes you compelled that this is going to happen

445
00:29:13,320 --> 00:29:19,880
